  "id": "amino-acid",
  "name": "Amino acid metabolism: nitrogen handling",
  "category": "metabolic",
  "topic": "metabolism",
  "summary": "Amino-acid nitrogen is funnelled to glutamate by aminotransferases (PLP-dependent), released as NH₄⁺ by glutamate dehydrogenase, carried safely between tissues as glutamine or alanine, and disposed of as urea. The carbon skeletons (pyruvate, oxaloacetate, α-ketoglutarate) feed the TCA cycle or gluconeogenesis.",
  "evidence": "established",
  "layout": "manual",
  "location": "Liver, muscle, kidney (cytosol and mitochondria)",
  "net": "Amino acid + α-KG ⇌ α-keto acid + glutamate;  glutamate + NAD(P)⁺ + H₂O → α-KG + NH₄⁺ + NAD(P)H",
  "clinical": "ALT and AST are clinical markers of liver damage. Phenylketonuria (phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency) is detected by newborn screening. The glucose–alanine cycle moves nitrogen from muscle to liver.",
